ying  sort of solidarity with the NHS and with  other frontline workers that we would  want to see but of course we keep things  under review in order to ensure that if  there are further steps they can be  implemented but what we have sought to  do is to make sure that we implement the  right steps at the right  time following appropriate scientific  advice the question was about what the  further levers could be and so what are  the further options that you're talking  about that the Prime Minister's talking  about I mean people really want straight  answers yes and I think we can take  further steps but it's important that  what am I thinking about something as  serious as this when we want to make  sure that we have the right steps in  place that were influencing people's  behavior in the right way that we are  keeping people safe that we don't  preempt discussion of what other steps  may be required but the Prime Minister  the whole government we follow the  advice that comes from our scientific  advisors from our medical advisers and  if there are appropriate steps that they  recommend that we take then we will take  them but it doesn't help those advisors  it doesn't help our preparations if we  preempt them before we need to at the  same time you're asking people to make  extraordinary changes to their lives  people are worried when they looking at  the figures that come out on a daily  basis showing the death toll ticking up  you won't tell us what the scientific  advice is what the working assumptions  are you won't tell us what the further  steps are that the government is  prepared to take  is there any direction that you can give  us on how long the lockdown for example  could last well at the moment it is the  case that as you say people are making  enormous sacrifices they are showing the  their sort of public spiritedness the  selflessness that is inspiring and the  best advice at the moment is for people  to observe the current guidelines and we  hope that that can flatten the curve  certainly all the evidence is that  people by observing those guidelines are  contributing to reducing the spread of  infection but it's also important to say  that the the scientific models are out  there people have had a chance to look  and to examine them the receive range as  people will know of potential outcomes  but those outcomes are not predetermined  influence those outcomes and if all of  us practice safe social distancing if  all of us stay at home then the number  of people who are being infected reduces  the resilience of the NH  can grow the NHS can become better  equipped to deal with this situation  the question was about how long the  lockdown could last for I'm still not  really that clear and professor Neil  Ferguson of Imperial one of the advisors  of course the government's following it  very closely  has said that he expects a lockdown to  last until early June is that a fair  expectation well as I say there are  different projections as to how long the  lockdown might last but it's not the  case that the the length of the lockdown  is something that is absolutely fixed it  depends on all of our behavior we can if  we follow the guidelines we can deal  more effectively with the spread of the  disease the models of projections and of  course we adjust our action on the basis  of behavior on the basis of data on the  basis of facts on the basis of science  but people should as the Prime  Minister's letter today  absolutely underlines and recognize that  the situation will get worse before it  gets better and that is why it is so  important that all of us play our part  it's also why it's so inspiring that  those leading the NHS have been able to  increase capacity increase the number of  beds available and ensure that we can be  in a better position to deal with the  increased level of infection and indeed  the increased level of mortality that is  coming talking about the NHS in  particular the government an