ge we've been working as I  say with scientists with you know the  big players in providing medical  supplies and drugs like boots and others  in order to increase the number of tests  that we have and the UK has been one of  those countries which has been testing  proportionately a large section of its  population and some other countries but  there is more to do much more to do so  it doesn't sound like you can tell me  when the full rollout will be and many  will be wondering why it has taken so  long and two weeks ago today the chair  of the British Medical Association dr.  John Magpul was on this program saying  about how important it was to get  testing for NHS staff I can't emphasize  how serious the problem is we don't have  the luxury of time anymore why is it  that two weeks on you still can't tell  us when  NHS staff are going to get tested what  we are increasing the number of tests  and that's why so much effort and energy  has been directed by scientists and by  others in order to increase the number  of tests that take place as I say 10,000  people being tested today that is a  significant increase over where we were  last week and that number is going to be  increasing all the time the other part  of the fight on the NHS of course is the  number of ventilators the number of  intensive care beds and I know that the  government's been trying to kind of ramp  up the availability of both of those  things and you started I think from a  position of having 5,000 ventilators so  how many ventilators and how many  intensive care beds are now available  well we have available to the NHS now  just over 8,000 ventilators that's  because we've repurposed some of the  intensive care capacity that we have in  the NHS it's also the case that Simon  Stephens the chief executive of the NHS  has done deal with the private sector in  order to increase ventilator capacity  available to the NHS and we are going to  get another 8,000 or so ventilators  including summer wh